  <content>
  <section><title> Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>The function \(W\) gives the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a pot of water on a stove \(t\) minutes after the stove is turned on. After 30 minutes, the pot is taken off the stove.</para>
<para>The graph of the function is shown.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="graph of temperature of water over time"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4c9e0f12217b960734c19d8111c00021400c1d4d"/></media></figure></para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Is 250 in the range of function \(W\)? Explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>No, 250 is not in the range. The highest temperature is 212 degrees Fahrenheit.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Describe the range of the function.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>The range is all values between 75 and 212, the lowest and the highest temperature; \(75\leq y\leq212.\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Does \(W(t)=0\) have a solution? Explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>No, a solution to this equation would be the time when the temperature is 0 degrees Fahrenheit, which is never the case.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
